#{[{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046660} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186553634} :activity/id #uuid "f3e06349-f9ac-5719-a7b9-44a6d3063919" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050539 :topic/id #uuid "52089b25-b5c5-5717-906a-bd765cce0502" :topic/math-academy-id 3119} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186553629 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186492844 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2284ef03-aeaa-5e50-9406-e44a27d2edd0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\\\ 0 \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\end{bmatrix}$\nand\n$\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186492286} {:answer/id #uuid "2fdbc765-9651-5639-8112-8a0f046be0ec" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}$\nand\n$\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186492319} {:answer/id #uuid "9d55135c-e6fc-540d-b590-5fdddcd8980b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\\\ 0 \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\end{bmatrix}$\nand\n$\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{\\sqrt{6}}{6} \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{6}}{3} \\\\ -\\frac{\\sqrt{6}}{6}\\end{bmatrix}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186492524} {:answer/id #uuid "ad2b44c2-eb45-57f5-a1dc-bce8c3dfce15" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\\\ 0 \\\\ -\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\end{bmatrix}$\nand\n$\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{\\sqrt{6}}{3} \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{6}}{6} \\\\ -\\frac{\\sqrt{6}}{6}\\end{bmatrix}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186492563} {:answer/id #uuid "e5691b04-e00a-5077-8a2d-e2eee3328fd5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}$\nand\n$\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186492662}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186492524} :answer-field/id #uuid "1deb190f-aaea-51df-a506-e53c0439123b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186492716}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "2f400860-7ffc-5147-852c-7cca9efa6b2f" :question/math-academy-id "q-94346" :question/problem "$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 2 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 3 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 2 & 3\\end{bmatrix},\\,D=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 7\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nConsider the matrices shown above. \nGiven that $D={P}^{T}\\,AP,$ where $P$ is an orthogonal matrix, which of the following could be the first and second columns of the matrix $P?$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that a matrix $A$ is orthogonally diagonalizable if there exists an orthogonal matrix $P$ such that\n\n$$\nA=PD{P}^{T},\n$$\n\nwhere $D$ is a diagonal matrix. Equivalently,\n\n$$\nD={P}^{T}AP.\n$$\n\nMoreover, a matrix $A$ is orthogonally diagonalizable if and only if it is symmetric.\n\nFirst, note that $A$ is symmetric and $D$ is diagonal. Therefore, $D={P}^{T}\\,AP$ gives an orthogonal diagonalization of $A.$\n\nFrom the diagonal matrix $D$ we deduce that the eigenvalues of the matrix $A$ are ${\\lambda }_{1}=1,$ ${\\lambda }_{2}=1,$ and ${\\lambda }_{3}=7.$ So, the first and second columns of $P$ correspond to the eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{1}=1.$ \n\nNow, we need to find an eigenvector that corresponds to ${\\lambda }_{1}={\\lambda }_{2}=1.$ Computing $A-I,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-I & =\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 2 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 3 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 2 & 3\\end{bmatrix}-\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 2 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 2 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 2 & 2\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(A-I)x=0$ gives two eigenvectors: \n\n$\\,$ ${v}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\\,$ and $\\,{v}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nNotice that eigenvectors ${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{2}$ are not orthogonal. So, we proceed using the Gram-Schmidt process. We set \n\n$$\n{e}_{1}={v}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\n\nand we find ${e}_{2}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}_{2} & ={v}_{2}-\\frac{{v}_{2}\\cdot {e}_{1}}{{e}_{1}\\cdot {e}_{1}}{e}_{1} \\\\  & ={v}_{2}-\\frac{1}{2}{e}_{1} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}-\\frac{1}{2}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ 1 \\\\ -\\frac{1}{2}\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, dividing ${e}_{1}$ and ${e}_{2}$ by their respective norms, we obtain the first and second columns of $P:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{e}_{1}}{‖{e}_{1}‖} & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\\\ 0 \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ \\frac{{e}_{2}}{‖{e}_{2}‖} & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{\\frac{3}{2}}}\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ 1 \\\\ -\\frac{1}{2}\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{\\sqrt{6}}{6} \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{6}}{3} \\\\ -\\frac{\\sqrt{6}}{6}\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "6ea43364-052a-50ae-ae32-c79c004285a5"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186553634 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186394963 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "515e7963-43a8-5d4c-b890-b12012ce6c51" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186394965} {:answer/id #uuid "28470fa3-1d40-50b2-a80d-1ee99e65ae81" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186394966} {:answer/id #uuid "d5588f7e-3d7a-589f-9e27-01f3829fb78d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3} \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3} \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186394967} {:answer/id #uuid "fa276602-eaa8-58fe-8046-b32c896de75b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3} \\\\ -\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3} \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186394968} {:answer/id #uuid "0092226f-56fc-51da-a4bb-a95cb3699695" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\\\ -\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186394969}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186394967} :answer-field/id #uuid "a2d3ea6d-180a-530e-a554-9eaf5810635a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186394964}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "5bac7e35-18fd-5d08-a4e1-72c5d7aa78ae" :question/math-academy-id "q-94266" :question/problem "$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & 3 \\\\ 2 & 2 & 2 \\\\ 3 & 2 & 1\\end{bmatrix},\\,D=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 6\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nConsider the matrices shown above. \nGiven that $D={P}^{T}\\,AP,$ where $P$ is an orthogonal matrix, which of the following could be the third column of the matrix $P?$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that a matrix $A$ is orthogonally diagonalizable if there exists an orthogonal matrix $P$ such that\n\n$$\nA=PD{P}^{T},\n$$\n\nwhere $D$ is a diagonal matrix. Equivalently,\n\n$$\nD={P}^{T}AP.\n$$\n\nMoreover, a matrix $A$ is orthogonally diagonalizable if and only if it is symmetric.\n\nFirst, note that $A$ is symmetric and $D$ is diagonal. Therefore, $D={P}^{T}\\,AP$ gives an orthogonal diagonalization of $A.$\n\nFrom the diagonal matrix $D$ we deduce that the eigenvalues of the matrix $A$ are ${\\lambda }_{1}=0,$ ${\\lambda }_{2}=-2,$ and ${\\lambda }_{3}=6.$ So, the third column of $P$ corresponds to the eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{3}=6.$ \n\nNow, we need to find an eigenvector that corresponds to ${\\lambda }_{3}=6.$ Computing $A-6I,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-6I & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & 3 \\\\ 2 & 2 & 2 \\\\ 3 & 2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}-6\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-5 & 2 & 3 \\\\ 2 & -4 & 2 \\\\ 3 & 2 & -5\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(A-6I)x=0$ gives the eigenvector \n\n$$\n{v}_{3}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\n\nwhere $‖{v}_{3}‖=\\sqrt{3}.$ Dividing ${v}_{3}$ by its norm, we obtain the third column of $P:$ \n\n$$\n\\frac{{v}_{3}}{‖{v}_{3}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3} \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3} \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "79d57286-dd25-5f24-aefd-354ef80c9c1e" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186553644}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186553644 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186492913 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "78197d70-00a7-5346-922d-ec6723bfe518" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186492460} {:answer/id #uuid "b44f0b5f-d418-56ed-b60f-63dd89b63865" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186492578} {:answer/id #uuid "e8cb6331-4637-5828-97e4-26ba566dbbee" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186492670} {:answer/id #uuid "ee276881-acfd-54fc-ade7-b03deecf2e2e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{2\\sqrt{5}}{5} \\\\ -\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{5} \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186492678} {:answer/id #uuid "f81e2cb1-6362-54d0-aabf-decb5246473d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\\\ 0 \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186492694}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186492694} :answer-field/id #uuid "dc95ee91-4a2e-557d-87e2-692ae59772de" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186492796}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "d9482400-abcf-59ec-ba2d-fb58a274b62b" :question/math-academy-id "q-94297" :question/problem "$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 3 & 3 \\\\ 3 & -1 & 3 \\\\ 3 & 3 & 3\\end{bmatrix},\\,D=\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 8\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nConsider the matrices shown above. \nGiven that $D={P}^{T}\\,AP,$ where $P$ is an orthogonal matrix, which of the following could be the second column of the matrix $P?$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that a matrix $A$ is orthogonally diagonalizable if there exists an orthogonal matrix $P$ such that\n\n$$\nA=PD{P}^{T},\n$$\n\nwhere $D$ is a diagonal matrix. Equivalently,\n\n$$\nD={P}^{T}AP.\n$$\n\nMoreover, a matrix $A$ is orthogonally diagonalizable if and only if it is symmetric.\n\nFirst, note that $A$ is symmetric and $D$ is diagonal. Therefore, $D={P}^{T}\\,AP$ gives an orthogonal diagonalization of $A.$\n\nFrom the diagonal matrix $D$ we deduce that the eigenvalues of the matrix $A$ are ${\\lambda }_{1}=-3,$ ${\\lambda }_{2}=0,$ and ${\\lambda }_{3}=8.$ So, the second column of $P$ corresponds to the eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{2}=0.$ \n\nNow, we need to find an eigenvector that corresponds to ${\\lambda }_{2}=0.$ Computing $A-0I,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-0I & =\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 3 & 3 \\\\ 3 & -1 & 3 \\\\ 3 & 3 & 3\\end{bmatrix}-0\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 3 & 3 \\\\ 3 & -1 & 3 \\\\ 3 & 3 & 3\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(A-0I)x=0$ gives the eigenvector \n\n$$\n{v}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\n\nwhere $‖{v}_{2}‖=\\sqrt{2}.$ Dividing ${v}_{2}$ by its norm, we obtain the second column of $P:$ \n\n$$\n\\frac{{v}_{2}}{‖{v}_{2}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\\\ 0 \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "8b9dd26e-078e-59b0-9131-9d71925a223f" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186553669}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186553667 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162625 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7b17109b-503d-5d39-8994-1c239e1d6f12" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}$\nand\n$\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ -\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\end{bmatrix}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355745} {:answer/id #uuid "8839af14-7b53-5d4e-bc41-0268ce08626d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3} \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}$\nand\n$\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3} \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3} \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}\\end{bmatrix}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355869} {:answer/id #uuid "acafb5b8-ec91-5d67-8ea0-125e56d52cd8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}$\nand\n$\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356192} {:answer/id #uuid "aecc46f1-2732-5322-92ae-9ab97cd8e884" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}$\nand\n$\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ \\frac{2\\sqrt{5}}{5} \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{5}\\end{bmatrix}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356205} {:answer/id #uuid "b2a5541d-7944-5a33-9a5b-efd6cfaa8758" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}$ \nand\n$\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356237}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186356205} :answer-field/id #uuid "b7490596-2fa1-53bc-8ada-e942488fb1b8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186357249}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "1d443cf2-e450-59f7-912e-8007eb24ac35" :question/math-academy-id "q-94332" :question/problem "$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 5 & -2 \\\\ 0 & -2 & 8\\end{bmatrix},\\,D=\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 9\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nConsider the matrices shown above. \nGiven that $D={P}^{T}\\,AP,$ where $P$ is an orthogonal matrix, which of the following could be the first and second columns of the matrix $P?$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that a matrix $A$ is orthogonally diagonalizable if there exists an orthogonal matrix $P$ such that\n\n$$\nA=PD{P}^{T},\n$$\n\nwhere $D$ is a diagonal matrix. Equivalently,\n\n$$\nD={P}^{T}AP.\n$$\n\nMoreover, a matrix $A$ is orthogonally diagonalizable if and only if it is symmetric.\n\nFirst, note that $A$ is symmetric and $D$ is diagonal. Therefore, $D={P}^{T}\\,AP$ gives an orthogonal diagonalization of $A.$\n\nFrom the diagonal matrix $D$ we deduce that the eigenvalues of the matrix $A$ are ${\\lambda }_{1}={\\lambda }_{2}=4,$ and ${\\lambda }_{3}=9.$ So, the first and second columns of $P$ correspond to the eigenvalues ${\\lambda }_{1}$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}.$ \n\nNow, we need to find eigenvectors that correspond to ${\\lambda }_{1}={\\lambda }_{2}=4.$ Computing $A-4I,$ we get  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-4I & =\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 5 & -2 \\\\ 0 & -2 & 8\\end{bmatrix}-4\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -2 \\\\ 0 & -2 & 4\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(A-4I)x=0$ gives two eigenvectors: \n\n$\\,$ ${v}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}\\,$ and $\\,{v}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nNotice that eigenvectors ${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{2}$ are orthogonal. Finally, dividing ${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{2}$ by their respective norms, we obtain the first and second columns of $P:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{v}_{1}}{‖{v}_{1}‖} & =\\frac{1}{1}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ \\frac{{v}_{2}}{‖{v}_{2}‖} & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ \\frac{2\\sqrt{5}}{5} \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{5}\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "c42ec998-95c8-5b02-9bfd-620df8c32a8c" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186553629}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186553669 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186505739 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5af1ce91-3b7a-534b-98ad-56dc4a10b93d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{22}} \\\\ \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{22}} \\\\ \\frac{3}{\\sqrt{22}}\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505287} {:answer/id #uuid "5e64e4d0-0be7-5588-9a76-c5376f91f8f7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{11}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{11}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{11}}\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505296} {:answer/id #uuid "6f89375f-1402-57bf-9d90-6e83a80103da" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505332} {:answer/id #uuid "71d799ca-d5f1-5bf0-a256-91e6f1e8a407" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{22}} \\\\ -\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{22}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{22}}\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505337} {:answer/id #uuid "a51a893a-d1e6-5946-9669-2b709310dc79" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{11}} \\\\ -\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{11}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{11}}\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505434}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186505287} :answer-field/id #uuid "82103b11-eb72-51ff-8fe5-77b1959844e9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505665}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "15341cc2-96e2-5aa8-b822-d1195f4412c9" :question/math-academy-id "q-94302" :question/problem "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 3 & 3 \\\\ 3 & -1 & 3 \\\\ 3 & 3 & 3\\end{aligned}],\\,D=[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 8\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the matrices shown above. \nGiven that $D={P}^{T}\\,AP,$ where $P$ is an orthogonal matrix, which of the following could be the third column of the matrix $P?$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that a matrix $A$ is orthogonally diagonalizable if there exists an orthogonal matrix $P$ such that\n\n$$\nA=PD{P}^{T},\n$$\n\nwhere $D$ is a diagonal matrix. Equivalently,\n\n$$\nD={P}^{T}AP.\n$$\n\nMoreover, a matrix $A$ is orthogonally diagonalizable if and only if it is symmetric.\n\nFirst, note that $A$ is symmetric and $D$ is diagonal. Therefore, $D={P}^{T}\\,AP$ gives an orthogonal diagonalization of $A.$\n\nFrom the diagonal matrix $D$ we deduce that the eigenvalues of the matrix $A$ are ${\\lambda }_{1}=-3,$ ${\\lambda }_{2}=0,$ and ${\\lambda }_{3}=8.$ So, the third column of $P$ corresponds to the eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{3}=8.$ \n\nNow, we need to find an eigenvector that corresponds to ${\\lambda }_{3}=8.$ Computing $A-8I,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-8I & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & 3 & 3 \\\\ 3 & -1 & 3 \\\\ 3 & 3 & 3\\end{aligned}]-8[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-5 & 3 & 3 \\\\ 3 & -9 & 3 \\\\ 3 & 3 & -5\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(A-8I)x=0$ gives the eigenvector \n\n$$\n{v}_{3}=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nwhere $‖{v}_{3}‖=\\sqrt{22}.$ Dividing ${v}_{3}$ by its norm, we obtain the third column of $P:$ \n\n$$\n\\frac{{v}_{3}}{‖{v}_{3}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{22}}[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{22}} \\\\ \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{22}} \\\\ \\frac{3}{\\sqrt{22}}\\end{aligned}]\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "c67e89a0-ed38-570d-b6f6-d2395f50c83c" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186553667}}] :activity/title "Diagonalization of 3x3 Symmetric Matrices" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186552867}]}
